53	Wellness Academy_chunk_0014	een the age of 12-15 years \nin girls compared to 14-17 years in boys (Davies et \nal., 2005). Almost half of the adult bone mass is \naccumulated during the growth spurt in adolescence \n(Parfitt, 1994).\n\n11\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nDiet modification is a recommended strategy for the \nmanagement of ADHD in children (Schnoll et al., 2003). \nA large-scale cohort study in children investigated the \neffect of diet on mental and behavioral performance. \nHigher diet quality was found to be associated \nwith reduced likelihood of behavioral performance \n(Kohlboeck et al., 2012). Therefore, a prudent diet \nwith high consumption of fruits and reduction of \nfoods high in sugar and fat content is recommended \nto prevent behavior problems in children, in addition \nto being better for overall health. While exercise is \nstrongly linked to cognitive health in the general child \npopulation, evidence suggests that exercise can also \nimprove cognitive performance in children with ADHD \n(Christiansen et al., 2019).\nMultiple observational studies document protection \nof mental health and better health-related quality of \nlife in children and adolescents by promoting physical \nactivity and limiting a sedentary lifestyle (Wu et al., \n2017; Rodriguez-Ayllon et al. 2019). Work by Hakala et \nal. (2019) shows the influence of physical activity on \ncognitive performance. Exposure to physical activity \nfrom childhood to early adulthood is associated with \nbetter midlife reaction time, whereas physical activity \nin young adulthood and adulthood is linked to better \nvisual processing and sustained attention (Hakala et al., \n2019). A number of studies have tried tracking various \nphysical activities from childhood and adulthood in \nan attempt to show the link between the continuity. A \ncross-sectional study conducted on women reported \na strong connection between physical activity at any \npoint over the life course and lower likelihood of \ncognitive impairment. This connection, however, was \nsignificantly stronger in women who were physically \nactive as teenagers (Middleton et al., 2010). Regular \nparticipation in sports during adolescence is associated \nwith a high level of physical activity in adulthood \n(Tammelin, et al., 2003). A Norwegian study suggested \nthat the number of activities partaken in during \nadolescence is comparably important for physical \nactivity later in life than the participation in any specific \nactivities (Kjønniksen et al., 2008). \nPoor nutrition and inadequate physical activity are \ntwo leading causes of rapidly increasing childhood \noverweight and obesity problem. Prevention of \nchildhood obesity is critical in guarding against weight-\nrelated chronic illnesses that could have negative short- \nand long-term health effects. Schools and governments \nglobally continue to put a strong focus on promoting \nphysical activity, with some parts of the world focusing \non early health-literacy as an effective measure.\nThe gut microbiome, its composition and  \nfunctionality are connected to childhood undernutrition \nand obesity (Pekmez et al., 2018). Modulation of the \nmicrobiome via prebiotics, probiotics and synbiotics \ncould be an effective prevention and treatment strategy \nto guard against both undernutrition and obesity. \nThe gut microbiome, its \ncomposition and functionality \nare connected to childhood \nundernutrition and obesity \n(Pekmez et al., 2018).	[0.01589937,0.017402885,-0.050455976,-0.012344299,-0.038336784,0.0031341815,-0.02035335,0.025847256,-0.0064881006,-0.012359953,-0.010356381,-0.0004378585,-0.055335067,0.011428768,-0.013751606,-0.047940087,0.0033128588,-0.0081,-0.00989114,-0.011765749,0.011115825,-0.017189527,0.04823897,0.030985642,0.022570858,0.012477152,0.032472227,-0.0063677276,-0.007180502,0.036191672,0.021415744,-0.00235541,0.033739507,-0.029862216,0.009417759,-0.01010429,-0.020431943,-0.06262969,