54	Wellness Academy_chunk_0015	tment strategy \nto guard against both undernutrition and obesity. The gut microbiome, its \ncomposition and functionality \nare connected to childhood \nundernutrition and obesity \n(Pekmez et al., 2018).\n\n12\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nYOUNG AND MIDDLE ADULTHOOD \nAdulthood begins around 19 years ago of age. Young \nand middle adulthood include significant life changes. Typically, by adulthood, the human body has reached \nits physical peak. Major organs have fully matured. Balanced nutrition during adulthood can assist in \nmaintaining an active and healthy life and promoting \nhealthy aging. Metabolic syndrome remails one of \nthe major health concerns during adulthood as one \ntakes on more responsibilities. Due to greater family \nand professional demands, there is an increased \nlikelihood of reduced physical activity and negative \nchanges in diet. These changes coupled with stress \nare associated with elevated health risks. It is during middle age when adults begin to \nexperience the first signs of aging. To maintain health \nand commence healthy aging, it is important to be \naware of the impact of lifestyle factors on health. Psychological stress is associated with unhealthy \ndietary choices (Oliver et al., 2000; Roberts, 2008; \nKhaled et al., 2020). Increase in cortisol level due to \nchronic stress is strongly linked to changes in food \nchoices resulting in increased energy consumption, \nincreased body weight and increased anxiety and \ndepression (Roberts, 2008). Stress-induced eating  \nmay also compromise health by increasing blood \npressure (Oliver et al., 2000). The overall pattern  \nof diet is considered as more important factor in  \nhealth and chronic disease risk than single foods  \nor food groups (Xu et al., 2020). Multiple correlational studies show a strong  \nconnection between physical activity and bone health \n(Borer, 2005). An observational study on young adults \nin Japan found a strong correlation between regular \nbreakfast consumption and ten hours of exercise per \nweek and higher bone mineral density (Ishimoto et al., \n2013). Increased physical activity during early  \nadulthood is associated with increased physical activity \nduring middle adulthood (Kraal et al., 2021). Physical \nactivity during adulthood is also associated with higher \ninitial memory levels and reduced risk of hypertension \nlater in life (Kraal et al., 2021). Increased physical activity \nis critical for maintaining cognitive health. This is also \ntrue in the case of adults with Down’s Syndrome that \nare prone to Alzheimer’s disease (Ptomey et al., 2018). Sleep deprivation during adulthood is strongly linked \nwith both physical and cognitive impairment (Honn  \net al., 2019). OLDER ADULTHOOD\nThe World Health Organization projects that by \n2050, 2.1 billion people globally will be 60 years and \nolder (WHO, 2022a). The aging process alters body \ncomposition and affects nutritional status. Early aging \nand associated geriatric diseases can be prevented \nand managed by nutrition and physical activity. There \nis a strong relationship between immunity and nutrition. Undernutrition makes one more susceptible to infection \nby weakening the immune defense. As a result, the \nimmune response to an infection can negatively  \nimpact nutritional status and body composition  \n(Calder, 2013). Aging may decrease immune \ncompetence (Calder, 2013), which increases the risk  \nof developing both nutrition related chronic illnesses, \nsuch as cardiovascular diseases and diabetes, \nand infections such as pneumonia and influenzas \n(Eggersdorfer et al., 2018). Addressing nutrient deficiency is important in old age.	[0.03823082,0.013841018,-0.041199826,-0.019914353,-0.051730126,-0.01262477,-0.022024779,0.014837871,0.0010758684,-0.0241973,-0.0017356885,-0.015897036,-0.09237651,0.0006016772,0.010615677,-0.021970907,-0.016493417,0.017184235,-0.02646147,0.023417393,