56	Wellness Academy_chunk_0017	in old \nage due to greater bone mineral density (Yang et al., \n2013). Higher peak bone mass achieved during early \nadult life reduces the risk of osteoporosis later in life \n(Demontiero et al., 2012).\n\n13\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nSkeletal muscle mass is a major determinant factor of \nquality of life and survival (Morton et al., 2018). It helps \nmaintain both physical and metabolic health (McKendy \net al., 2020). Poor metabolic health is a limiting factor \nto healthy aging. Age-related losses in skeletal muscle \nmass and function are a serious public health concern \n(Sakuma and Yamaguchi, 2012). Defined as Sarcopenia, \nthe age-related loss of skeletal muscle mass results \nin deteriorated muscle quality and quantity, affecting \nmovement, strength, and increasing the risk of frailty \nand fall related injuries (Sakuma, K., & Yamaguchi, \n2012). Sarcopenia brings a lot of physical limitations. It \nis also believed to contribute to bone loss (Demontiero \net al., 2012). It is estimated that it occurs in 13-24% in \npeople under 70 years of age and more than 50% in \nthose over 80 (Baumgartner et al., 1998). While there are several factors involved like nutritional intake, oxidative \nstress and hormonal stress, the majority of the evidence points at anabolic resistance as one of the main causes of \nloss of muscle during aging. Anabolic resistance is a reduced muscle protein synthesis response to dietary protein \nor amino acids (Moore 2014; Morton et al., 2018). While aging aggravates muscle anabolism, there is evidence that \nsedentary lifestyle and physical inactivity can instigate it, likely at any age (Moore 2014; Morton et al., 2018). Experts \nhave long suggested higher protein consumption to stimulate muscle anabolism. Morton et al (2018) recommend \nolder individuals to ingest at least 1.2 g of protein/kg/day in addition to incorporating exercise three times a week, \nusing both heavy and light loads. Some supplemental strategies are suggested to counteract sarcopenia, like \nomega 3 fatty acids, amino acid leucine, ursolic acid and creatinine (Sakuma and Yamaguchi, 2012; McKendry et al., \n2020). Resistance training is considered as one of the most effective non-pharmacological method for age related \nsarcopenia. It can help increase skeletal muscle mass, strength and improve physical function (McKendry et al., \n2020). A study conducted on Polish respondents above 15 years of age found that nearly 40% of respondents do not \nmeet WHO’s recommendation on physical activity (Biernat and Piątkowska, 2018). These include both unemployed \nand professionals between 50-64 years of age without children and retirees 65 and above without children. This \nnumber reverberates with the 2014 Eurobarometer survey on sport and physical activity, where 59% of European \nUnion respondents either exercise occasionally or never (European Commission, 2014). Increased physical activity \nhelps maintain mitochondrial function in aging skeletal muscle (Seo et al., 2016). Engaging in physical activity may \nalso reduce the risk of premature mortality (Zhao et al., 2014). Our understanding of the functional and structural changes in an aging brain is continually expanding. Several \nfactors are implicated in the aging of the brain. The structure and metabolic pathways within the brain gradually \nchange with age (Moore et al., 2018). Decline in the levels of neurotransmitters dopamine and serotonin, hormonal \nchanges including growth and sex hormones, production of reactive oxygen species (ROS), mitochondrial \ndysfunction, calcium dysregulation and impaired glucose metabolism all have been associated with the aging brain \nand its declining cognitive performance (Peters, 2006).	[0.051655684,0.02552432,-0.06395633,-0.0334548,-0.027548652,-0.04235913,0.012558611,0.031563647,0.028247766,-0.014732617,-0.008622314,0.018960126,-0.0608485,0.004260745,0.0020265249,-0.027626455,-0