57	Wellness Academy_chunk_0018	species (ROS), mitochondrial \ndysfunction, calcium dysregulation and impaired glucose metabolism all have been associated with the aging brain \nand its declining cognitive performance (Peters, 2006).\n\nIncreased oxidative stress and antioxidant deficiencies can \nbe risk factors for cognitive decline (Berr et al., 2000). The most common cognitive change observed with aging is \nmemory (Peters, 2006). There is a significant reduction in the volume of both gray and white matter, which is similar \nin both men and women (Resnick et al., 2003). A decline in white matter integrity is associated with age related \ncognitive decline and Alzheimer’s disease (Wang et al., 2020). Physical activity, however, is believed to have positive \neffects on the structure of white matter (Wolf et al., 2020). It is also linked to positively influencing cognitive function \nin patients with dementia (Groot et al., 2016). Resistance training, done twice a week, shows promise as an approach \nto changing the trajectory of cognitive decline in the elderly with mild cognitive impairment (Nagamatsu et al., 2012). There is evidence that supports the brain’s intake of certain nutrients like vitamins B, D and E and omega 3 fatty \nacids to aid in maintaining cognitive health (Tucker, 2016). A study involving French women’s diets identified the \npotential long-term impact of their nutritional choices to deter age-related cognitive decline; the study found soluble \ndietary fiber as an important dietary component associated with lower cognitive decline (Vercambre et al., 2009). While there are lot of studies that have evaluated the impact of nutraceutical ingredients on cognition later in life, \nthere are limited long-term studies that provide compelling evidence to support cognitive improvement in the \nelderly. Future studies that consider nutrition, genetic makeup and lifestyle will be helpful in determining the impact \nof active nutrition on cognition later in life. Our understanding of the \nfunctional and structural \nchanges in an aging brain  \nis continually expanding. Several factors are \nimplicated in the aging  \nof the brain.	[-0.009462477,0.026249677,-0.033223405,-0.038668036,-0.03696009,-0.047234684,-0.01398179,0.028223006,0.0428805,-0.006914471,-0.013868586,-0.021251285,-0.06632485,0.013228596,0.0053385408,-0.03116068,-0.015732644,-0.04061372,-0.05835382,0.022829425,0.008262852,-0.0014325329,0.021541744,-0.016290484,0.0055421344,0.027835151,-0.032690167,-0.017088033,0.025268793,0.049886577,-0.012906391,-0.034122933,0.023834836,-0.016620573,-0.015781365,0.006299619,0.038878586,-0.05403484,-0.042206857,0.015775787,-0.021636339,-0.0034851416,0.026545987,-0.020679511,0.017997837,-0.025145954,-0.014717486,-0.006007724,0.005512754,-0.067346066,-0.023255339,-0.001787524,0.04012405,-0.013255324,-0.02184458,0.053126767,-0.066882536,0.011604199,-0.062000297,-0.0006593365,0.024120133,-0.0052237567,-0.013657413,-0.059145436,-0.001987686,0.0432349,0.010286929,0.017881999,-0.022483863,-0.054530002,-0.0072508063,0.027359104,-0.018258883,-0.025179213,-0.017201312,0.0018914382,-0.031393893,0.010371854,0.02506516,0.01782554,0.008947663,0.013011138,-0.01652372,0.0023291572,-0.005956535,0.018234026,-0.027227992,0.015886396,0.050690547,-0.050943606,0.00063955074,-0.048602365,0.011528614,-0.048327263,0.0050746473,-0.0049417363,-0.020638114,-0.060642198,0.076002695,-0.000667594,-0.0060707685,0.012266182,-0.027857497,-0.012968873,0.022550724,0.018354002,-0.0089492705,-0.0067668613,0.030092908,0.00039922708,0.035249397,0.0027971899,0.0035342658,0.062030878,0.023898775,-0.024798512,-0.0023365647,-0.035352647,0.019446276,0.019404484,0.007465503,0.032847226,0.07786489,0.0077718524,0.011510436,0.032902397,0.059014313,0.0018487956,-0.00073190755,-0.0033666885,0.029134866,-0.028061254,-0.070717536,-0.026595507,-0.019315144,0.016615732,0.028522179,0.05309094,-0.001864462,-0.064375915,0.009101744,0.014680448,-0.010999708,-0.028250962,0.0