42	Wellness Academy_chunk_0003	population (Bird et al., 2017)\nThere are several factors that influence nutrient  \nneeds. These include age, gender, genetic  \nconditions, physical activity, etc.\n\n3\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nThe epigenetic effects of diet and exercise are well \nstudied. The stretches of DNA connected to the end \nof chromosomes called telomeres get shorter with age \nand contribute to cell apoptosis. Telomeres have a \nstrong link with aging. A healthy diet is associated with \nlonger telomere length (Ekmekcioglu, 2020). Increased \nphysical activity is also linked to longer telomere length \nin various populations, particularly in older adults \n(Arsenis et al., 2017). This suggests the role of both \nnutrition and exercise in aging at the cellular level. \nNutrition during  \npre-natal and early \npost-natal life has the \npotential to program \nlong-term health  \nand wellness. \nWHO recommends 60 minutes of moderate to  \nvigorous intensity exercise daily for children and  \nyouth between 5 and 17 years. The recommendation \nfor everyone over 18 years is 150 minutes of moderate \nexercise or 75 minutes of vigorous exercise weekly \nor a combination of both.  The benefits based on \nthese recommendations include cardiorespiratory \nand muscular fitness, bone health, cardiovascular and \nmetabolic health biomarkers and reduced symptoms of \nanxiety and depression (WHO, 2010). Practicing yoga \nis known to significantly reduce inflammatory cytokines \nand improve quality of life of patients with rheumatoid \narthritis (Gautam et al., 2020). \nEFFECT OF HEALTHY NUTRITION AND PHYSICAL ACTIVITY ON OVERALL HEALTH \nRoutine maintenance of an active lifestyle throughout \nlife is important. Diet and physical activity are known  \nto deliver greater benefits to overall health together \nthan separately (Witard and Ball, 2018). The changes \ninitiated by exercise can be modulated via nutrition  \n(Jeukendrup, 2017).  \nNutrition during pre-natal and early post-natal life has \nthe potential to program long-term health and wellness. \nLikewise, physical activity earlier in life is associated \nwith long-term health benefits. \nSuboptimal nutrition is a risk factor for various \nchronic health conditions which are also correlated \nwith depression (Berk and Jacka, 2012). Physical \ninactivity and a sedentary lifestyle pose a serious \nproblem for public health. Physical activity or inactivity \ncan be influenced by various factors, either social, \nphysical, biological, behavioral, physiological, or \nenvironmental (Sallis et al., 2000; Hirvensalo and \nLintunen, 2011). WHO reports physical inactivity as \nthe fourth leading risk factor for mortality globally \n(WHO, 2010). Physical activity tends to change with \nlife stage. Although unproven, it is assumed that as \ndemands and motivators change, it can impact the \nbehavioral aspect of participation in physical activities \n(Corder et al., 2009). Physical exercise can also be a \nnon-pharmacological intervention to manage chronic \npain for certain conditions (Ambrose and Golightly, \n2015). Research suggests that exercise is a potential \ntherapeutic treatment for various physiological and \nbehavioral disorders such as hypertension, addiction, \ndepression and anorexia (Thorén et al., 1990).  \nIncreased physical activity has negative correlation  \nto metabolic diseases such as hypertension, diabetes \nand heart diseases (Kraal et al., 2020). Diabetes and \nprediabetes are known to accelerate cognitive decline \nin older adults (Marseglia et al., 2019), which makes \nphysical activity an effective strategy to manage both \nphysical and cognitive health.	[-0.0010144298,0.009310259,-0.06525738,-0.0005688263,-0.052891016,-0.04354268,-0.00092664215,0.0069453656,0.00019098906,-0.012592848,-0.012943515,0.018685415,-0.058559246,-0.008866137,0.009369605,-0.028404027,-0.0026786353,-0.020305378,-0.025057057,-0.009808494,0.019583777,-0.023278786