43	Wellness Academy_chunk_0004	nd \nprediabetes are known to accelerate cognitive decline \nin older adults (Marseglia et al., 2019), which makes \nphysical activity an effective strategy to manage both \nphysical and cognitive health.\n\n4\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nMENTAL AND COGNITIVE HEALTH\nCorrelation between diet and mental health is well \nstudied. A large-scale population done on California \nresidents in the U.S. found lower consumption of fruits \nand vegetables and increased consumption of fast \nfood, soda and French fries was associated with both \nmoderate and severe psychological distress (Banta \net al., 2019). Another small-scale study determined a \nnegative correlation between a vegetarian diet and \ndepression in adults over 18 years of age (Palyngan  \net al., 2021).  \nCognition is mental processing involved in acquiring \nknowledge, learning, attention, intelligence, memory \nand consciousness (Dauncey, 2009). Brain structure \nand function are dependent on nutritional intake \nand many aspects of nutrition have been implicated \nin mental health and cognitive health including \ndepression, ADHD, dyslexia, dementia, Alzheimer’s  \nand Parkinson’s disease (Dauncey, 2009).  \nRegulation of blood glucose is associated with \nimprovement in the quality and duration of intellectual \nperformance. Likewise, poor glycemic control across \nall ages is associated with lower cognitive performance \n(Bourre, 2006; Benton and ILSI Europe, 2008). Iron \ndeficiency in early life is also known to interfere with \ncognitive development (Benton and ILSI Europe, 2008). \nThe importance of good nutrition cannot be \nundermined. A cohort study associated smaller \nhippocampal volume to both higher intake of unhealthy \nfoods and lower intake of nutrient-dense foods (Jacka \net al., 2015). The hippocampus is the component of the \nbrain that plays a major role in learning and memory. \nIt is vulnerable to various lifestyle factors including \nundernutrition and micronutrient gaps (Lodygensky \net al., 2008). Hippocampal volume is also linked to \ngreater serum levels of brain derived neurotrophic \nfactor (BNDF), a neurotrophin associated with reduction \nin age related cognitive decline (Erickson et al., 2011). \nExercise induced changes in the brain are well documented. Physical exercise is coupled with increased \nhippocampal volume (Erickson et al., 2011), improved memory (Northey et al., 2018) and reduction in brain aging \n(Best et al., 2015). Aerobic exercise training is effective at reversing hippocampal volume loss in late adulthood \n(Erickson et al., 2011). It is effective in combating brain aging by hippocampal neurogenesis (Erickson et al., 2011) \nand reduction in inflammation (Colbert et al., 2004). Data suggest an evolutionary relationship between the \nbrain size and exercise (Raichlen and Gordon, 2011) and the increase in exercise capacity has been linked to \nincreased brain size in mammals.\nExercise effects core human body functions on a molecular level. It can influence neuroplastic pathways  \n(Repple and Opel, 2021) and can be an effective intervention for various physiological and mental health  \nissues. Neuroplasticity is the brain’s capacity to adapt, change and modify both structure and function \nthroughout life to ever changing conditions (Voss et al., 2017; di Liegro et al., 2019). It is a continuous process \nwhereby neurons modify their connections in response to changes in both internal and external stimuli (Lin et \nal., 2018). This activity is essential for healthy functioning of the brain, although it can be manipulated in both \nhealthy and unhealthy brains (Voss et al., 2017).	[-0.009772401,0.013349304,-0.010166189,-0.01331494,-0.04544309,-0.011302301,-0.015963264,0.027764726,0.022861946,-0.016510893,-0.008353821,-0.03522186,-0.06561519,0.013391476,-0.0022368059,-0.04867468,-0.012331576,-0.0112584885,-0.020603817,0.0021733781,-0.028465493,-0.014771503,0.0379713