44	Wellness Academy_chunk_0005	nal and external stimuli (Lin et \nal., 2018). This activity is essential for healthy functioning of the brain, although it can be manipulated in both \nhealthy and unhealthy brains (Voss et al., 2017).\n\n5\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nMALNUTRITION\nAccording to the World Health Organization (WHO), \nbattling all forms of malnutrition is one of the biggest \nglobal challenges. WHO’s global strategy for women’s, \nchildren’s and adolescents’ nutrition (2016-2030) is \naimed at ending all forms of malnutrition by 2030 by \naddressing the nutritional needs of children, adolescent \ngirls, and pregnant and lactating women (WHO, 2015). \nOptimal nutrition can influence the changes in brain \nstructure and function. Moderate undernutrition is \nassociated with lack of cognitive function. Preterm \ninfants with intrauterine growth restriction have lower \nhippocampal volume, resulting in less-mature brain \nfunction, compared to full-term infants (Lodygensky et \nal., 2008). Preterm infants that were given high nutrient \nformulas showed a higher IQ during adolescence \ncompared to standard formula-fed preterm infants \n(Isaacs et al., 2008). Prenatal malnutrition is an \nimportant factor in adult health and disease. There is \ncompelling evidence that links maternal malnutrition to \nincreased risk of long-term behavioral and psychotic \nissues including schizophrenia (Susser et al., 2008).\nDuring old age, nutrition priorities change. There is \nless energy requirement, increased nutrient need to \nminimize lean muscle loss, sarcopenia and cachexia \n(Agarwal et al., 2013). There are several factors that \nplay in the development of undernutrition in older \nadults. Malnutrition in older age results in poorer \nhealth outcomes. In addition to various physiological \nlimitations, it can result in a decline in mental function, \nreduced quality of life and increased mortality (Phillips \net al., 2010; Robinson, 2017). \nMITOCHONDRIAL FITNESS\nMitochondrial fitness is essential to overall health. In \naddition to producing ATP, mitochondria also assist \nin conversion of nutrients into building blocks for \nsignaling and apoptosis (Catic, 2019). Deregulation of \nmitochondrial function affects immunity and multiple \naspects of aging including age-related illnesses such \nas Type 2 diabetes and Alzheimer’s (Jornayvaz and \nShulman, 2010; Xia et al., 2019). A healthy body is \ncapable of creating mitochondria on its own through \na process called mitochondrial biogenesis. There \nare several nutrients associated with mitochondrial \nbiogenesis including B vitamins, ascorbic acid, \nvitamin E, zinc and selenium (Wesselink et al., 2019). \nDownregulation of mitochondrial biogenesis in the \nwhite adipose tissue is one of the main causes of \nobesity-associated metabolic diseases (Heinonen et \nal., 2020). White adipose tissue plays a key role in \nenergy and metabolism. Any form of exercise is linked \nwith re-energizing the mitochondria. Mitochondrial \nbiogenesis can be increased by exercise and caloric \nrestriction (Jornayvaz and Shulman, 2010). Mitochondria \nfunction slows down during aging which impacts \nenergy production and can contribute to poor muscle \nstrength and sarcopenia (Rea, 2017). Physical activity \nincreases mitochondrial content in muscle (Jornayvaz \nand Shulman, 2010). \nOptimal nutrition can  \ninfluence the changes in  \nbrain structure and function. \nModerate undernutrition is \nassociated with lack of  \ncognitive function.	[0.0100281695,0.003918101,-0.05171776,-0.032783303,-0.035291936,0.0015049987,0.0013967485,0.0026945628,0.011240218,-0.009364933,-0.029371204,-0.01465375,-0.06421669,-0.00541057,0.014041677,-0.046449326,-0.021179996,-0.010082849,-0.05647383,-0.000302381,-0.021928784,-0.042813834,0.05099324,0.03700107,-0.009633768,0.029576296,0.04579367,-0.0040923124,-0.004746515,0.007231012,0.012120539,-0.007429466,0.019740421,-0.03611077,0.021065693,-