45	Wellness Academy_chunk_0006	Optimal nutrition can  \ninfluence the changes in  \nbrain structure and function. Moderate undernutrition is \nassociated with lack of  \ncognitive function.\n\n6\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nMETABOLIC DISEASES AND INFLAMMATION \nNutrition and lifestyle, mainly over-nutrition and sedentary life, encourage the progression and pathogenesis \nof obesity and metabolic diseases. Nutritional status can modulate metabolic inflammation, a root cause of \nobesity, Type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular diseases (Kirwan et al., 2017). Adenosine Monophosphate activated \nprotein kinase (AMPK) is an enzyme that plays an important role in energy metabolism and can regulate cellular \nhomeostasis that affects inflammatory and metabolic pathways (Kirwan et al., 2017). Obesity can impact AMPK  \nand whole-body metabolism. High levels of inflammation are known to inhibit AMPK activation. Monounsaturated \nfatty acids, berberine, Alpha lipoic acid, resveratrol, curcumin, flavonoids are known to help in AMPK activation  \n(Lyons and Roche, 2018). \nThere are various factors that have been implicated in triggering chronic inflammation, including chronic  \ninfections; diet; lifestyle-induced obesity; microbiome dysbiosis; and social, cultural and environmental changes \n(Miller et al., 2011). Chronic inflammation is the biggest cause of mortality globally (Furman et al., 2020). Ischemic \nheart disease, stroke, cancer, diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) \nand autoimmune and neurodegenerative conditions are responsible for more than 50% of deaths worldwide \n(GBD 2018). Chronic inflammation developed during childhood can persist throughout life and negatively affect \noverall health (Miller et al., 2011). The clinical repercussions of systemic chronic inflammation can increase the risk \nof various metabolic disorders, including hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, depression, osteoporosis and \nsarcopenia (Miller et al., 2011).  \nPhysical exercise is considered an important non-pharmacological tool to attenuate the progression of metabolic \ndiseases (Mathias et al., 2014). It provides protection against obesity and obesity related co-morbidities in older \npopulations (Papadopoulou et al., 2020). Obesity has been identified as a factor in motor and cognitive impairment \nthroughout the lifespan (Wang et al., 2016).  Depression and anxiety have been reported as comorbidities of  \nobesity (Simon et al., 2006). \nPGC1α, a transcriptional coactivator that can facilitate energy metabolism, control mitochondrial biogenesis and \noxidative metabolism in many cell types, is known to be involved in the aging process (Austin and St-Pierre, 2012). \nIt is induced in the muscle by exercise and stimulates several beneficial effects of exercise in and beyond the \nmuscle. One of them is the release of the hormone Irisin from the skeletal muscles via the Fndc5 gene. Boström et \nal. (2012) discovered the hormone Irisin that can increase energy expenditure, glucose homeostasis and negatively \nimpact obesity. Circulating Irisin levels are relatively low in sedentary individuals and increased in physically active \nindividuals (Arhire et al., 2012; Boström et al., 2012). Given that exercise is beneficial to prevent metabolic diseases, \nit is reasonable to believe that Irisin also plays an important role in disease prevention.	[0.02094439,-0.0025714221,-0.027109835,-0.02666956,-0.013220406,-0.046018586,-0.016113576,0.012246121,0.0018740011,0.0011316246,-0.036212195,-0.02019629,-0.084106945,0.011051402,0.030310871,-0.02387472,0.010658965,-0.022142041,-0.044253197,0.000553832,-0.03256237,-0.019391002,0.030749911,0.003944676,-0.014347027,0.01830973,-0.00088789564,0.032413203,0.02623469,0.022690346,0.008230804,-0.01280587,0.008623007,-0.0331736,0.007536583,0.014562804,0.025854355,-0.060731404,-0.030779283,0.03691028,-0.005747885,-0.021507397,-0.024452923,-0.044004