46	Wellness Academy_chunk_0007	rhire et al., 2012; Boström et al., 2012). Given that exercise is beneficial to prevent metabolic diseases, \nit is reasonable to believe that Irisin also plays an important role in disease prevention.\n\n7\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nBONE HEALTH\nBone quality is an important determinant of health \nthroughout life. Lifestyle choices, chiefly diet and \nexercise are known to influence 20-40% of adult  \npeak bone mass, a strong indicator of bone health \n(Weaver et al., 2016). They are beneficial to bone  \nhealth at all ages and their positive effect is  \nextensively studied. Bone mineral density increases \nthrough childhood and adolescence and reaches  \npeak bone mass by adulthood (Lu et al., 2016). More \nthan 99% of total body calcium in the human body is \nin the skeleton. During rapid periods of bone growth, \nsuch as childhood and adolescence, inadequate \nintakes of both calcium and vitamin D (required \nfor calcium absorption) can contribute to low peak \nbone mass and increase fracture risk both during \nand later in life. Peak bone mass attained during \nyouth is a strong predictor of osteoporosis risk later \nin life (Weaver et al., 2016). Evidence suggests that \npredisposing factors to osteoporosis can be seen in \nchildren (Davies et al., 2005). Weight bearing, high impact exercises are linked \nto increased bone density (Falk et al., 2007; Falk et \nal., 2010; Tenforde and Fredericson, 2011). Athletes \nshow a 20% increase in both bone mineral content \nand density compared to non-athletes (Willems et \nal., 2017). A combination of proper nutrition, vitamin \nD supplementation and resistance exercise during \nspaceflight reduced the expected loss in bone mineral \ndensity in astronauts (Smith et al., 2012) highlighting \nthe synergy of nutrition and physical activity even in \nextreme conditions. Excessive body fat percentage is \nnegatively related to bone health (Khwanchuea and \nPunsawad, 2021). SLEEP, STRESS AND  \nDEPRESSIVE DISORDERS\nSleep is critical for physiological and cognitive \nfunctions. Sleep deprivation is known to weaken \ncognitive performance, mood, appetite regulation, \nglucose metabolism and immunity (Halson, 2014). Various studies have examined the influence of  \nnutrition on sleep. Diet is directly associated with \nsleep quality (Gaona-Pineda et al., 2021). Diets rich \nin protein may improve sleep quality while diets high \nin carbohydrates and fat may negatively affect sleep. High carbohydrates in diet can lead to shorter sleep \nlatencies, while high fat is associated with total sleep \ntime (Halson, 2014). This could be one of the reasons \nwhy the Mediterranean style diet is linked to better \nsleep duration (Castro-Diehl et al., 2018). Both sleep quality and quantity are important for  \noverall health. It is no secret that exercise also  \npromotes sleep quality. Physical exercise is linked to \nsignificant improvements in overall sleep quality and \nsleep latency (Kelley and Kelley, 2017). Stress is known to negatively affect the  \nhippocampal volume (Lodygensky et al., 2008),  \nthus affecting cognitive health. Healthier food \nchoices and increased physical activity are effective \nstress reducing strategies. A survey that examined \nrelationships among stress, physical activity and diet \namong employees in a workplace, found significant \ndifferences in perceived stress in groups that engaged \nBone quality is an  \nimportant determinant  \nof health throughout life.\nin regular exercise and made healthier food choices \n(Gillan et al., 2013). Engagement in moderate exercise \nwas connected to improved resilience to stressful \nevents and healthier coping styles. There is evidence that nutrition plays an important role \nin the prevention and treatment of behavioral disorders \n(Kris-Etherton, 2021).	[0.04151657,-0.0043712435,-0.058748674,0.009108803,-0.010836449,-0.037928693,-0.0016742508,0.034390494,-0.03108309,-0