48	Wellness Academy_chunk_0009	ts role in prevention of ischemic \ncardiovascular diseases (Bourre, 2006; Grosso et al., \n2014). Physical exercise is also a proven strategy for \nattenuating depression (Belvederi Murri et al., 2015).\n\n8\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nUNDERSTANDING THE CHANGING \nPHYSICAL AND COGNITIVE LANDSCAPE \nTHROUGHOUT THE LIFE CYCLE\nPREGNANCY, INFANCY AND EARLY CHILDHOOD\nThe human body undergoes a series of physiological \nchanges during pregnancy. Optimal nutrition prior \nto conception and during pregnancy is important to \nmeet the body’s altered demands. Adequate nutrition \nprevents various nutritional imbalances that can \nnegatively affect pregnancy and fetal growth and \nexert important short-term and long-term effects on \nboth the mother and baby. During reproductive years, \nwomen are prone to high risk of vitamin and mineral \ndeficiency (Bird et al., 2015). During pregnancy, this \nrisk may be further elevated due to plasma volume \nexpansion (Mathews et al., 2004). Maternal nutrition has a strong influence on fetal \ndevelopment. Maternal macronutrient excess has \nbeen linked to a greater long-term risk for adult \ndisease in the fetus (Zhang et al., 2011). The link \nbetween maternal nutrition and chronic metabolic \ndisease in children has been well recognized (Morea \net al., 2013). In addition, new evidence from both \nhuman and animal studies shows a direct correlation \nbetween paternal obesity (during conception) and \noffspring health (Billah et al., 2022). There is a strong connection between obese \nchildbearing women, obese pregnancies and \nincreased rate of obesity in their children (Koletzko  \net al., 2012). Overnutrition during pregnancy promotes \nglucose and fat supply to the fetus that can result \nin hyperinsulinemia and increased fetal fat deposits \n(Koletzko et al., 2012). Fetal exposure to trans fatty \nacids is associated with adverse health effects and \nincreased risk of metabolic diseases’ development in \nlife (Mennitti et al., 2014). High intake of saturated fatty acids is associated \nwith insulin resistance and diabetes in mothers \nand consumption of long chain polyunsaturated \nfatty acids such as arachidonic acid, EPA and DHA \ndeliver several health benefits in fetal development \n(Mennitti et al., 2014). Severe micronutrient imbalance \nis known to interfere with pregnancy outcomes and \nsupplementation is recommended in women with \ndeficits (Ballestín et al., 2021). The health impact of \nmicronutrients deficiencies is not as perceptible as \nenergy or protein undernourishment; however, it can \nbe momentous on both physical and cognitive health. Micronutrient supplementation in pregnancy is heavily \nresearched and adopted globally. It commonly includes \nvitamins A, D, E, folate, B12, B6, and C, iron, zinc, \niodine, copper, and selenium (Gernard et al., 2016). Calcium supplementation is associated with lowered \nrisk of preeclampsia and hypertensive disease during \npregnancy (Lowensohn et al., 2016). Safety and   \nefficacy of several supplements other than key \nnutrients, including vitamins and minerals are not well \nestablished for use during pregnancy. All essential nutrients are important for a healthy \npregnancy, however, Beluska-Turkan (2019) identify \neight key nutrients necessary during pregnancy, \npostpartum and through the 2nd birthday. These \ninclude carotenoids (lutein and zeaxanthin), choline, \nfolate, iodine, iron, omega-3 fatty acids and vitamin \nD. (Beluska-Turkan et al., 2019). The first 1,000 days \nof life, starting conception through 2 years of age are \na crucial for healthy growth, development and life \n(Beluska-Turkan et al., 2019). During the same time, \nbrain development is rapid.	[0.015692249,0.019533044,-0.05501605,0.025982942,-0.042097915,0.015640114,0.0043586446,0.015364692,-0.023043318,-0.0057759923,-0.04363713,-0.026430985,-0.08893039,0.0020438782,-0.0059575164,-0.064092316,-0.00773