50	Wellness Academy_chunk_0011	rtum (Guo et al., 2021). Type of \nexercise during pregnancy has long been a topic of \nOptimal nutrition prior  \nto conception and during \npregnancy is important  \nto meet the body’s  \naltered demands.\n\n9\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\ndiscussion. Clinical trials have shown that moderate \nto vigorous resistance exercise during pregnancy \nimproves physical fitness and does not endanger \nmaternal and fetal health (de Oliveria Melo et al., 2012; \nPetrov Fieril et al., 2015). In addition, sleep during \npregnancy is known to influence fetal growth and \ndevelopment (Moreno-Fernandez et al., 2020). \nOptimization of infant nutrition has lifelong beneficial \neffects on brain structure and function (Dauncey, \n2009). Human and animal studies have revealed the \nimportance of nutrition and lifestyle in the fetus and \ndevelopment of chronic diseases later in life. Birth \nweight and linear growth during the first two years are \nlinked to many beneficial outcomes throughout life \n(Adair et al., 2013).\nHuman brain development begins soon after \nconception and continues throughout early adulthood. \nDuring the first 1,000 days, the brain develops rapidly, \nFigure 1: The role of nutrition during pregnancy and early life development. Source: Beluska-Turkan et al 2019\nand nutrition plays an important part in its development \n(Nyaradi et al., 2013; Black, 2018). Early nutrition \nhas lifelong effects on brain structure and function. \nDocosahexaenoic acid (DHA), a long chain poly \nunsaturated fatty acid is the predominant fatty acid in \nthe brain, and there is sufficient evidence that knows its \nimportance in brain development (Bourre et al., 2006; \nde Souza et al., 2011). Its supplementation in infants \nshows improved language and communication skills \n(Willatts, 2018). DHA is associated with psychomotor \ndevelopment, eye-hand coordination, stereo acuity, \nbetter academic performance and ADHD prevention \n(Singh, 2005). There is also a growing body of evidence \nthat it can influence planning, working memory and \nattention control (Willatts, 2018). Consumption of poly \nunsaturated fatty acids during pregnancy is associated \nwith reduced risk of premature birth (Koletzko et al., \n2008). Figure 1 highlights the role of nutrition during \npregnancy and early life. \nFIRST 1000 DAY NUTRITION MATTERS\nTHE PHYSIOLOGICAL IMPLICATIONS OF NUTRITION\nDay 25\n100\n140\n280\nBIRTH\n310\n370\n640\n1000\n1st Trimester \n2nd Trimester \n3rd Trimester \n0-3 Months \nPostpartum \n3-12 Months \nPostpartum \n12-24 Months \nPostpartum \n+/- Hormone Levels \n+ Metabolic rate \n+ Oxygen Demand \n+ Blood Volume \n+ Nutrient Demands \n+ Cardiac Output \n+ Hormone Levels\n- Insulin Sensitivity\n+ Calcium Absorption\n+ Plasma Volume\n- Sleep\nBreastfeeding Support \nPostpartum Recovery \nRisk of Depression \nChanges in Hormones\n- Calorie Intake \n+ Stress & Lack of Sleep\nBack and Head Aches \nRestored Muscle Tone  \nand Connective Tissue\nContinued Breastfeeding  \n& Nutrition Support\nExercise \nBalanced Diet\nKey Nutrients \nFloate | Iodine | Vitamin D\nKey Nutrients \nCarotenoids | Folate  \nIron | Omega-3\nKey Nutrients \nCalcium | Carotenoids \nCholine  | Iodine | Iron \nOmega-3 | Vitamin D\nPostpartum \nCalcium | Chlorine | Folate | Iron | Omega-3s | Vitamin D\nMATERNAL CHANGES AND KEY NUTRIENTS \nTHE CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM \nMajor functional and minor \nstructural defects can occur \nMajor developmental  \ndefects can occur\nOPTIMAL SYNAPTOGENESIS AND REFINEMENT \nDURING EARLY DEVELOPMENT \nNewborn \n1 Month \n6 Months \n2 Years	[0.0018641308,0.024561504,-0.049207024,-0.00059463544,-0.053396456,0.001044678,-0.015728988,0.029518217,-0.0062646843,-0.032943074,-0.036578305,-0.028504733,-0.089431114,0.012812335,-0.00083025143,-0.057596926,-0.014185104,-0.020488903,-0.030654065,0.014168879,-0.0065320334,-0.0029752855,0.043568984,0.026361527,-0.00044453464,0.038189683,-0.004447166,0