51	Wellness Academy_chunk_0012	Major functional and minor \nstructural defects can occur \nMajor developmental  \ndefects can occur\nOPTIMAL SYNAPTOGENESIS AND REFINEMENT \nDURING EARLY DEVELOPMENT \nNewborn \n1 Month \n6 Months \n2 Years\n\n10\nWellness Academy | The Need for Active Nutrition\nCHILDHOOD AND ADOLESCENCE\nBoth childhood and adolescence are critical life stages \nfor physical, mental, social and intellectual health. This \nis the time in life where a lot of cognitive and social-\nemotional skills are processed to shape future mental \nand social health. Having a balanced diet during \nchildhood and adolescence is crucial to reducing health \nproblems and contributes to healthy aging. Compared to their body size, the recommended macro \nand micronutrient needs for children are higher than \nadults. Current data show that children do not meet \nthe recommended targets for a healthy diet (Corkins \net al., 2016). Children and adolescents are affected by \nmultiple forms of malnutrition (Best et al., 2010; Akseer \net al., 2017). There is a positive association between \nnutritional supplementation in childhood with executive \nfunction and mental well-being in adulthood (Ramírez-\nLuzuriaga et al., 2021). A number of observational \nand interventional studies have highlighted the \nimportance of diet on mental health. According to \nWHO, globally, 13% of 10- to 19-year-olds experience \na mental disorder, which makes it one in every seven \nchildren or adolescents. Mental limitations can cause \nphysical illnesses, among others, if not addressed \n(WHO fact sheets, 2022b) and limit the opportunities to \nlead a satisfactory adult life. Studies have also shown \nstrong correlation of academic performance to healthy \nnutrition and physical activity (Donnelly et al., 2016; \nAsigbee et al., 2018). Adolescence is a period of fast growth, second only to \nthe growth during infancy (Das et al., 2017). It is a critical \nperiod of significant physiological, sexual, neurological \nand behavioural changes. Because of the rapid growth,  \nthere is an increased nutritional vulnerability and \noptimal nutrition is of utmost importance. On average, \nthe nutritional requirements are greater during puberty \ndue to increased height and body weight, increased \nbone and muscle mass, increase in the size of various \norgans and expansion of blood volume (Corkins et \nal., 2016). The beginning of chronic illnesses can \nbe seen during adolescence (Corking et al., 2016). While overnutrition can result in obesity, nutritional \ndeficiencies during adolescence can result in long-term \nhealth problems, including delayed sexual maturation, \nloss of final adult height, osteoporosis, hyperlipidemia \nand obesity (Wahl, 1999). It is also speculated that \ndietary intake has a potential role in determining the \ntiming of puberty (Villamor and Jansen, 2016). Physical education and activities (including recreational \nsports) implemented at an early stage promote an \nactive lifestyle. Increased childhood physical activity is \nlinked to high adult bone density that can contribute \nto healthier aging without the risk of osteoporosis \n(Specker and Binkley, 2003). The development of \nbone mineral density and content during childhood \nand adolescence is implicated in attenuation of \nosteoporosis later in life (Ondrak and Morgan, 2007). Many studies have measured sport specific effects on \nbone strength. Participation in all sports and physical \nactivity is beneficial for bone health. However, intensive \ntraining in high-impact, weight-bearing activities \nshow enhanced bone mineral density in children and \nadolescents (Falk et al., 2010) compared to non-impact \nactivities. Active participation in soccer or football \npositively affects whole body bone mineral density \nin children and adolescents (Lozano-Berges et al., \n2018).	[0.042045526,0.025059525,-0.063642904,-0.019166108,-0.05897404,0.010095939,-0.016989449,0.0079812,-0.0135