109	The Peter Attia Drive - AMA #72: Fasting: benefits for body composition and disease prevention, potential risks, and Peter's updated practical framework_chunk_0004	of 16 to 18 is what most people are doing there, and then finally long-term fasting where you just have a number of days stacked up, so again that's probably a helpful way to orient people to fasting.\n\nJust one interesting historical piece of context, my book, I think it has two chapters on nutrition, there was originally a section in there that I thought was still in there, and when we were preparing for this podcast I went back. to look at it and I'm like, where is it, where is it, where is it? and I realized we cut the entire thing out. In the part of the book that I wrote about fasting, there was an entire section I had written about the world's longest recorded fast, which was by this guy named Agus Barbiri, who was a Scottish fellow in the late 60s, dramatically overweight, probably in the ball park of about 450 pounds, but did a medically supervised fast, forget this, 382 days, so 380. two days he was hospitalized, consuming nothing but tea, coffee, sparkling water, vitamins and yeast extract. over that period of time, over a year, he lost 276 pounds, taking him down to a body weight at discharge from the hospital, if my memory serves me correctly of about 170, 175 pounds, and what was really interesting is there was an article written about in a medical journal, it might have even been the new england journal of medicine, roughly six or seven years post discharge from the hospital, and he still maintained a normal weight, he might have been about 10 pounds heavier than he was at the time of his discharge, which was a completely normal weight for him, so again it was just a very interesting story and of course speaks to the... remarkable resilience, provided there's enough fat to metabolize. I do love the idea of if we asked you how many chapters were in your book and you had a list out, you 