106	The Peter Attia Drive - AMA #72: Fasting: benefits for body composition and disease prevention, potential risks, and Peter's updated practical framework_chunk_0001	Considering this is Ama-72, we've talked a lot about stuff in between then, before we've dedicated a full episode to it.\n\nSince then, there's been a lot of things that have changed on how you think about this, your opinions evolved, we've kind of talked about it here and there, but it's also a topic we get asked about so much, and there's so many people who talk about this in different ways, and I think it creates some confusion, and you've written about. the book, you do it with yourself, you have patients who do it, so we kind of gathered all these questions and said, all right, let's dedicate a full episode to this, and in it we'll first start by just identifying the different terms, i think sometimes what causes confusion is people may say the term fasting or different versions of fasting and mean it in different ways, so once we identify that we're then going to focus a lot of it on what are the reasons that people may fast, what are the reasons that they see benefits, anything from body comp metabolic health, to just general lifespan hopes, to cardiovascular disease, cancer prevention, treatment, neuro, alzheimer's, parkinsons, autoimmune symptoms, arthritis and more, so a lot of different things that will talk about the possible benefits, we'll also look at some possible negative consequences that people should be aware of, and then hopefully wrap it up in kind of a nice framework for people to apply it to themselves and understand how you apply it in your own life, and also how you apply with so all that said, anything you want to add before we get rolling? no, I don't think so. I mean, obviously I'd love to spend some time talking about Formula 1, but I don't think we're going to have time with that aggressive agenda. do Formula One drivers do long-term fasting, do you know? I don't know any who do. well, then that might be the extent of F1 in this podcast. also, have you been to your kids school recently? any elementary kids have strong opinions on fasting that you heard of recently, no, but I know who to ask, so there you go, something tells me that kid's got some opinions on this, I would love to hear what they are, rolling into it, I always like to kind of do this every now and then, because one thing about you is you're not afraid to change your opinion, so when new evidence comes out, you're not afraid to be like, okay, how does that fit in the scaffelding of how I thought about this before, and where does that change, and we usually do it in major episodes, like episode 200, 300 called strong convictions loosely held on what you've changed your opinion on, and this is a topic where I think your opinion has evolved a little bit as new evidence has come out, as you've seen it in practice, and I think it's always important for people to understand why you feel so strongly about changing your opinion and the ability to change your opinion, because sometimes in the space people get really wet to what they think and they'll dig their heels in, and when people change opinions, you'll see comments like, well how could you say this if you used to say this? and i think it's always helpful for people to hear from you as we set the stage, why do you think it's so important for scientists, doctors to be able to change their opinion? Well, honestly, I think it's important for everybody to be able to change their mind or change their opinion in light of new information. I also realize that we live in a world today where that's quite difficult. There's a reason I would never be able to be a politician. I think politics is an area where it's very difficult to change your mind and maintain credibility.	[0.015230368,-0.017376628,-0.022268726,-0.005432119,-0.010828421,-0.092217706,0.028038869,0.006536851,0.05277309,-0.02434732,-0.013327945,0.0111489855,-0.02263186