108	The Peter Attia Drive - AMA #72: Fasting: benefits for body composition and disease prevention, potential risks, and Peter's updated practical framework_chunk_0003	If I did that, I would be out of energy balance, I would be metabolically unhealthy, and I would deal with all the sequele of that.\n\nSo, I can't give you a number as to what people are in my bucket, but experiencially, through taking care of patients for more than a decade, it appears to be more than 80% of people are in my category, where they have to pay some attention, and so the framework that I divised around this was this: idea of DR, TR and CR, and you have to be doing generally at least one of those things, so DR means dietary restriction, so you deliberately restrict some element within the diet, the most common applications of this are typically certain macronutrients, so there are people who restrict carbohydrates, there are people who restrict fat, there are people who restrict animal products, there are people who restrict maybe processed foods, there's no right way to do this, there are lots of way to do it, and any form of d that is significant enough generally produces the desired effect. the second area is tr or time restriction. this is what people loosely refer to as intermittent fasting or daily time restriction, where you just say, hey, look, not really going to pay attention or dramatically change what i eat or even pay attention to how much i'm eating when i'm eating, but i'm really going to restrict the period of time in which i eat. the narrower i make my eating window, the more i'm going to probably restrict overall calories. finally there's CR which says, actually what i'm going to do is just focus on minimizing my total caloric intake over the course of the day, and i'm going to do this not necessarily by paying attention to what i'm eating or when i'm eating, just always focusing on eating less, obviously one can choose to mix these. when I was at my absolute lightest and leanest, which was necessary when I was racing a bicycle, I was basically going very hardcore on DR and TR, so I was highly restrictive as to when I ate, I was basically eating one snack and one meal a day, and I was being insanely restrictive on what I was eating uh, and not surprisingly I way... 16 17 pounds less than I do today, so lots of different ways to do this. now the problem, as it pertains to our discussion today, which is about fasting, is that we don't really have clean definitions of this. the term fasting applies to a diverse set of feeding patterns, but it's a general definition, and it can range from anything like only consuming water for days at a time to what I just described a moment ago, which was time restriction, which is, hey, i'm just going to kind of not eat for 16 or 18 hours a day. so i think as we go through this discussion, nick, we'll certainly shed more light on the different types of protocols and maybe the pros and cons of each. yeah, and maybe kind of as a follow-up from there, let's just cover what are the most common types of fasting, so when people are talking about fasting, do you just kind of want to list out what those are? and i think we actually have a graph that we look at internally that we can throw up, which should be helpful here. sure. as I'm talking, maybe look at what's sitting over my shoulder here, so intermittent fasting, I kind of referred to that a moment ago, that's the intermittent abstinence of food from anywhere, depending on who you read, I typically refer to it as sort of 12 to 24 hours, some of the literature says actually it could be 12 to 36 hours, and I think it depends on whether you're referring to time restricted eating or TRE, during which period of time food intake is limited to... periods of time within a day, or something called ADF, which is alternative day fasting, where you alternate a day of regular feeding and a day of not feeding, so again, if you look at this graph or this figure, you can see what an ADF protocol might look like, and again the pink squares on this over the course of a seven day week show you days of either complete or significant caloric restriction, so you could see in the ADF you've got blue day on day one, the five and seven, so just normal ad libitum feeding, and then day two, 4,6, you've got anywhere from zero to 25% caloric intake depending on how aggressive you want to be. contrast that with periodic fasting where you have just a period of normal ad libibitum days, followed by a couple of successive days, could be more than one of course, of complete chloric restriction. time-restricted eating on the other hand, each day has a period of caloric restriction. in my view, it's very hard to call it TRE if it's than 16 hours, although I guess technically people might consider it TRE if it's less than 12, but I would say sort of 16 to 18 is what most people are doing there, and then finally long-term fasting where you just have a number of days stacked up, so again that's probably a helpful way to orient people to fasting.	[-0.0031337305,-0.022316873,-0.03815885,-0.012112666,-0.023742883,-0.056719366,-0.0042893896,0.01590407,0.010776711,0.010488302,-0.016170502,-0.012935567,-0.008463763,0.031640876,0.030169582,0.004888453,-0.024581775,-0.037273914,0.0006385522,-0.008566642,0.0149139855,0.031734034,0.028745512,-2.1176078e-05,0.005041006,0.0407975,-0.019503294,0.013113542,0.027334394,0.05979138,-0.004839859,0.017966257,0.007966385,-0.059536938,0.047131658,0.035686612,0.034568105,-0.021345133,-0.020267334,0.01558968,-0.011278659,-0.014946279,-0.001084614,-0.04358813,0.061741322,-0.036246605,-0.045119062,-0.0317401,-0.030005228,-0.03368534,0.01671717,-0.024063718,0.010253982,-0.037097536,0.00049195264,0.037478127,-0.01267908,0.0019660331,-0.04453204,-0.014680144,-0.0031964371,0.011295571,0.02694145,-0.020957876,-0.0032472005,0.044575453,0.045527045,-0.03207271,-0.020954784,-0.053683836,-0.017279917,0.02123356,0.029212393,-0.06726163